    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d316">

                <pb facs="0674" n="634" xml:id="pg634"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">784A.5/4–1151: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Israel</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State
                    </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d316-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tel Aviv</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-04-11T18:00:00+03:00">April 11, 1951—6
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>645. Having heard that this govt was planning an official celebration in
                    Jerusalem on May 11, I took occasion recently to review briefly the policy set
                    forth in Deptel 340, February 14<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d316-f2"/> when on a routine visit to the FonOff to make arrangements for the entertainment of official
                    visitors. This was done to forestall if possible receiving an official
                    invitation to celebration or at least prepare a basis for non-attendance shld
                    one nevertheless be recd.</p>
                <p>I have now been asked informally whether I wld consider it consistent with our
                    policy to attend such a celebration in Jerusalem and have expressed the view
                    that it wld not be consistent with such policy. I thanked the FonOff spokesman for taking matter up in this
                    way and said I hoped no invitation wld be reed in circumstances. I understand my
                    Brit, French and Belgian colleagues agree this view.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Davis</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
